          "source_text_hash": "sha256:cb63bad50fb73eec4806db512387fdc7a22fde4299dbfb31bc3d777241d09137",
          "source_structure_hash": "sha256:75ab72b1657c2864dbd61eecf788b21d473c20c5818c1eb80a3881b0f98a7adf",
          "source_math_hash": "sha256:44136fa355b3678a1146ad16f7e8649e94fb4fc21fe77e8310c060f61caaff8a"
        },
        {
          "schema_version": 1,
          "unit_id": "tag:072K:prose-0001",
          "parent_tag": "072K",
          "chapter": "stacks-introduction",
          "node_kind": "paragraph",
          "risk_level": "R2",
          "source_commit": "a04446e57ec1fbc252a871afcec7752fb2807b14",
          "source_text": "\n\n<STRUCT_0001>\nHere is what we are going to do:\n<STRUCT_0002>\n<STRUCT_0003> Start with your favorite category of schemes <MATH_0001>.\n<STRUCT_0004> Add a new symbol <MATH_0002>.\n<STRUCT_0005> A morphism <MATH_0003> <TEXTBFOPEN_0001>is<TEXTBFCLOSE_0001> an elliptic curve\n<MATH_0004> over <MATH_0005>.\n<STRUCT_0006> A diagram\n<MATH_0006>\n<TEXTBFOPEN_0002>is<TEXTBFCLOSE_0002> commutative if and only if there exists a morphism <MATH_0007>\nof elliptic curves over <MATH_0008>. We say <MATH_0009> <TEXTITOPEN_0001>witnesses<TEXTITCLOSE_0001>\nthe commutativity of the diagram.\n<STRUCT_0007> Note that commutative diagrams glue as follows\n<MATH_0010>\nbecause <MATH_0011> witnesses the commutativity of the outer\ntriangle if <MATH_0012> and <MATH_0013> witness the commutativity of the left\nand right triangles.\n<STRUCT_0008> The composition\n<MATH_0014>\nis given by <MATH_0015>.\n<STRUCT_0009>\nAt the end of this procedure we have enlarged the category <MATH_0016>\nof schemes with exactly one object...\n\n<STRUCT_0010><STRUCT_0011>\nExcept that we haven't defined what a morphism from <MATH_0017>\nto a scheme <MATH_0018> is. The answer is that it\nis the weakest possible notion such that compositions make sense.\nThus a morphism <MATH_0019>\nis a rule which to every elliptic curve <MATH_0020> associates a morphism\n<MATH_0021> such that given any commutative diagram\n<MATH_0022>\nthe diagram\n<MATH_0023>\nis commutative also. An example is the <MATH_0024>-invariant\n<MATH_0025>\nwhich you may have heard of. Aha, so now we're done...\n\n<STRUCT_0012><STRUCT_0013>\nExcept, no we're not! We still have to define a notion of morphisms\n<MATH_0026>. This we do in exactly the\nsame way as before, i.e., a morphism\n<MATH_0027>\nis a rule which to every elliptic curve <MATH_0028> associates\nanother elliptic curve <MATH_0029> preserving commutativity of\ndiagrams as above. However, since I don't know of a nontrivial example\nof such a functor, I'll just define the set of morphisms from\n<MATH_0030> to itself to consist of the identity for now.\n\n<STRUCT_0014><STRUCT_0015>\nI hope you see how to add other objects to this enlarged category.\nSomehow it seems intuitively clear that given any ``well-behaved''\nmoduli problem we can perform the construction above and add an object\nto our category. In fact, much of modern day algebraic geometry takes\nplace in such a universe where <MATH_0031> is enlarged with countably many\n(explicitly constructed) moduli stacks.\n\n<STRUCT_0016><STRUCT_0017>\nYou may object that the category we obtain isn't a category because\nthere is a ``vagueness'' about when diagrams commute and which combinations\nof diagrams continue to commute as we have to produce a witness\nto the commutativity. However, it turns out that this, the idea of having\nwitnesses to commutativity, is a valid approach to <MATH_0032>-categories!\nThus we stick with it.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n",
          "source_status": "CURRENT",
          "placeholders": {
